IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea-cpc-interface`
IMPORT `ea-types`

-- Evidence Act 1893, Part 3 "Examination of witnesses" and "Improper admission and rejection of evidence":
-- sections 137 to 169.
-- Source: source/EA1893.txt lines 3968 to 4545.
--
-- The questions this module answers: HOW IS A WITNESS EXAMINED (137 to 146), WHEN MAY A WITNESS'S PREVIOUS
-- STATEMENT BE USED, AND FOR WHAT (147, 157, 159), WHAT MAY BE ASKED TO TEST CREDIBILITY (148 to 155),
-- WHAT CORROBORATES (158 to 160A), HOW MEMORY IS REFRESHED (161 to 163), AND WHAT THE COURT MAY DO
-- (164 to 169).  Section 147 and section 157 are the two the Criminal Procedure Code's section 259(1)(a) and
-- (b) refer to by number; section 8(2) illustrations (j) and (k) and section 159 are where a complaint
-- about an offence is dealt with.  Section 138 (the court decides admissibility) is in ea-relevancy.l4.

§ `137 to 142. Order of examination`

-- 137: the order in which witnesses are produced and examined is regulated by the law and practice relating to
-- civil and criminal procedure respectively and, in the absence of any, by the discretion of the court.  That
-- sends the order to the Criminal Procedure Code and the Rules of Court and decides nothing here.

-- 139: examination by the party who calls the witness is examination-in-chief; by the adverse party,
-- cross-examination; by the party who called the witness after cross-examination, re-examination.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 139(1), (2) and (3)"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the examination is examination-in-chief` IF
    x's `the examination is by the party who called the witness`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 139(2)"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the examination is cross-examination` IF
    x's `the examination is by the adverse party`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 139(3)"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the examination is re-examination` IF
    x's `the examination is of a witness who was cross-examined and is then examined by the party who called the witness`

-- 140(2): examination and cross-examination must relate to relevant facts, but cross-examination need not be
-- confined to the facts the witness testified to in chief.  140(3): re-examination is to be directed to the
-- explanation of matters referred to in cross-examination; if new matter is introduced by permission of the court
-- the adverse party may further cross-examine on it.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 140(2)"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the examination or cross-examination satisfies section 140(2)` IF
    x's `the examination relates to relevant facts`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 140(3)"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the re-examination satisfies section 140(3)` IF
        `the examination is re-examination` x
    AND (   x's `the re-examination is directed to the explanation of matters referred to in cross-examination`
         OR x's `new matter is introduced in re-examination by permission of the court`)

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 140(3)"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the adverse party may further cross-examine on new matter` IF
    x's `new matter is introduced in re-examination by permission of the court`

-- 141: a person summoned to produce a document does not become a witness by producing it and cannot be
-- cross-examined unless called as a witness.  142: witnesses to character may be cross-examined and re-examined.
-- 140(1) and (4) (the order of examinations, and recall of a witness) go to the course of the trial and are inert here.

§ `143 to 145. Leading questions`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 143"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the question is a leading question` IF
    x's `the question suggests the answer which the person putting it wishes or expects to receive, or suggests disputed facts as to which the witness is to testify`

-- 144(1): leading questions must not, if objected to by the adverse party, be asked in an examination-in-chief or
-- re-examination, except with the court's permission.  144(2): the court shall permit them as to matters introductory or
-- undisputed, or which have in its opinion been sufficiently proved.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 144(1) and (2)"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the leading question must not be asked` IF
        `the question is a leading question` x
    AND (   `the examination is examination-in-chief` x
         OR `the examination is re-examination` x)
    AND x's `the adverse party objects to the leading question`
    AND NOT x's `the court permits the leading question`
    AND NOT x's `the question is as to a matter which is introductory or undisputed, or which has in the court's opinion already been sufficiently proved`

-- 145(1): leading questions may be asked in cross-examination, subject to (a) the question must not put into the mouth of
-- the witness the very words the witness is to echo back, and (b) it must not assume that facts have been proved which have
-- not been, or that particular answers have been given contrary to the fact.  145(2): the court may, in its discretion,
-- prohibit leading questions to a witness who shows a strong interest or bias in favour of the cross-examining party.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 145(1) and (2)"
GIVEN x IS AN `Examination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the leading question may be asked in cross-examination` IF
        `the question is a leading question` x
    AND `the examination is cross-examination` x
    AND NOT x's `the question puts into the mouth of the witness the very words which the witness is to echo back`
    AND NOT x's `the question assumes that facts have been proved which have not been proved, or that particular answers have been given contrary to the fact`
    AND NOT x's `the court has prohibited leading questions to a witness who shows a strong interest or bias in favour of the cross-examining party`

§ `146. Evidence as to matters in writing`

-- 146: a witness may be asked whether a contract, grant or other disposition of property was contained in a document, and
-- if the witness says it was, or is about to make a statement about the contents of a document which in the court's opinion
-- ought to be produced, the adverse party may object until the document is produced or facts are proved entitling the
-- party to give secondary evidence.  The Explanation: a witness may give oral evidence of statements made by other persons
-- about the contents of documents if those statements are in themselves relevant facts.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 146 and Explanation"
GIVEN m IS A `Matters in Writing Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the adverse party may object under section 146` IF
        (   m's `the witness says that a contract, grant or other disposition of property as to which the witness is giving evidence was contained in a document`
         OR m's `the witness is about to make a statement as to the contents of a document which in the opinion of the court ought to be produced`)
    AND NOT m's `the evidence is of statements made by other persons about the contents of documents, and the statements are in themselves relevant facts`
    AND NOT m's `the document has been produced, or facts have been proved which entitle the party who called the witness to give secondary evidence of it`

§ `147. Cross-examination as to previous statements`

-- 147(1): a witness may be cross-examined as to previous statements made by the witness in writing or reduced into
-- writing, and relevant to matters in question, without the writing being shown to the witness or proved; but if it is
-- intended to contradict the witness by the writing, the witness's attention must, before the writing can be proved, be
-- called to those parts of it which are to be used for the purpose of contradicting.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 147(1)"
GIVEN p IS A `Previous Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the witness may be cross-examined on the previous written statement` IF
        p's `the previous statement was made in writing or reduced into writing`
    AND p's `the previous statement is relevant to matters in question in the proceeding in which the witness is cross-examined`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 147(1)"
GIVEN p IS A `Previous Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the previous written statement may be proved to contradict the witness` IF
        `the witness may be cross-examined on the previous written statement` p
    AND p's `it is intended to contradict the witness by the writing`
    AND p's `the witness's attention was called, before the writing is proved, to the parts of it which are to be used for contradicting the witness`

-- 147(2): if a witness, on cross-examination as to a previous ORAL statement relevant to matters in question and
-- inconsistent with the present testimony, does not distinctly admit having made it, proof may be given that the witness
-- did make it; but before proof can be given, the circumstances of the supposed statement, sufficient to designate the
-- particular occasion, must be mentioned to the witness and the witness asked whether he or she made it.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 147(2)"
GIVEN p IS A `Previous Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the previous oral statement may be proved under section 147(2)` IF
        p's `the previous statement was oral`
    AND p's `the previous statement is relevant to matters in question in the proceeding in which the witness is cross-examined`
    AND p's `the previous statement is inconsistent with the witness's present testimony`
    AND p's `the witness does not distinctly admit that the witness made the oral statement`
    AND p's `the circumstances of the supposed oral statement, sufficient to designate the particular occasion, were mentioned to the witness and the witness was asked whether the witness made it`

-- 147(3): where a previous inconsistent or contradictory statement made by a person called as a witness is proved by virtue of
-- section 147, it is by virtue of subsection (3) admissible as evidence of any fact stated in it of which direct oral
-- evidence by the person would be admissible.  This is the statement "admitted under section 147" in CPC s 259(1)(a): the
-- statement is evidence of the facts stated, not merely of inconsistency.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 147(3)"
GIVEN p IS A `Previous Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the previous statement is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under section 147(3)` IF
        (   `the previous written statement may be proved to contradict the witness` p
         OR `the previous oral statement may be proved under section 147(2)` p
         OR p's `the previous inconsistent or contradictory statement is proved by virtue of section 147`)
    AND p's `direct oral evidence of the fact stated by the maker would be admissible`

-- 147(4) and (5): where a witness is cross-examined on a document used to refresh memory, the document may be made evidence;
-- and where a document or part is received in evidence by virtue of (4), any statement in it by the person using it to refresh
-- memory is admissible as evidence of any fact stated of which direct oral evidence by that person would be admissible.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 147(4) and (5)"
GIVEN p IS A `Previous Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement in the memory-refreshing document is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under section 147(5)` IF
        p's `the witness is cross-examined on a document used by the witness to refresh the witness's memory`
    AND p's `the document is received in evidence by virtue of section 147(4)`
    AND p's `direct oral evidence of the fact stated by the maker would be admissible`

-- 147(6): in estimating the weight, regard is to be had to all the circumstances, in particular whether the statement was made
-- contemporaneously and whether the maker had an incentive to conceal or misrepresent the facts.  Weight, not admissibility:
-- inert here.
-- 147(7): despite any written law or rule of practice requiring evidence to be corroborated or regulating the manner in which
-- uncorroborated evidence is to be treated, a statement admissible by virtue of this section is not capable of corroborating
-- evidence given by the maker of the statement.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 147(7)"
GIVEN p IS A `Previous Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement is incapable of corroborating the maker's evidence under section 147(7)` IF
        (   `the previous statement is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under section 147(3)` p
         OR `the statement in the memory-refreshing document is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under section 147(5)` p)
    AND p's `a written law or rule of practice requires the evidence given by the maker of the statement to be corroborated or regulates the manner in which it is treated if uncorroborated`

§ `148 to 155. Questions to test credibility`

-- 148: subject to section 154A, when a witness may be cross-examined the witness may be asked any questions tending (a) to
-- test accuracy, veracity or credibility; (b) to discover who the witness is and the witness's position in life; (c) to shake
-- the witness's credit by injuring character, although the answer might tend to criminate or expose the witness to a penalty
-- or forfeiture.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 148(a), (b) and (c)"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the question is lawful in cross-examination under section 148` IF
        q's `the witness may be cross-examined`
    AND (   q's `the question tends to test the witness's accuracy, veracity or credibility`
         OR q's `the question tends to discover who the witness is and what the witness's position in life is`
         OR q's `the question tends to shake the witness's credit by injuring the witness's character`)

-- 149: if such a question relates to a matter relevant to the suit or proceeding, section 134 applies, and the witness cannot
-- refuse on the ground of self-incrimination.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 149"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 134 applies to the question under section 149` IF
        `the question is lawful in cross-examination under section 148` q
    AND q's `the question relates to a matter relevant to the suit or proceeding`

-- 150(1): if a question relates to a matter not relevant to the suit or proceeding, except insofar as it affects the credit of
-- the witness by injuring character, the court is to decide whether the witness is to be compelled to answer.  150(2): the
-- court has regard to (a) such questions are proper if the truth of the imputation would seriously affect the court's opinion as
-- to credibility on the matter to which the witness testifies; (b) improper if the imputation relates to matters so remote in
-- time or of such character that its truth would not, or would only slightly, affect that opinion; (c) improper if there is a
-- great disproportion between the importance of the imputation and the importance of the witness's evidence; (d) the court may
-- draw from a refusal to answer the inference that the answer would be unfavourable.  The decision whether to compel is the
-- court's, so the rule states when the considerations make the question proper or improper.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 150(2)(a), (b) and (c)"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the question is proper under section 150(2)` IF
        q's `the imputation conveyed is such that its truth would seriously affect the court's opinion as to the witness's credibility on the matter to which the witness testifies`
    AND NOT q's `the imputation relates to matters so remote in time or of such character that its truth would not affect, or would affect only slightly, the court's opinion`
    AND NOT q's `there is a great disproportion between the importance of the imputation and the importance of the witness's evidence`

-- 151: no such question ought to be asked unless the person asking has reasonable grounds for thinking the imputation is well
-- founded.  152: where the court is of the opinion that a question was asked without reasonable grounds, it may, if asked by
-- an advocate or solicitor, report the circumstances to the Supreme Court (a discretion, inert here).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 151"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the question may be asked under section 151` IF
    q's `the person asking the question has reasonable grounds for thinking that the imputation is well founded`

-- 153: the court may forbid any questions it regards as indecent or scandalous, although they may have some bearing on the
-- questions before the court, unless they relate to facts in issue or to matters necessary to be known to determine whether the
-- facts in issue existed.  154: the court is to forbid any question that appears intended to insult or annoy, or which though
-- proper in itself appears to the court needlessly offensive in form.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 153"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may forbid the question as indecent or scandalous under section 153` IF
        q's `the question is indecent or scandalous`
    AND NOT q's `the question relates to a fact in issue or to a matter necessary to be known in order to determine whether the facts in issue existed`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 154"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court is to forbid the question under section 154` IF
    q's `the question appears to the court to be intended to insult or annoy, or appears needlessly offensive in form although proper in itself`

-- 154A: in criminal proceedings where the accused is charged with a sexual offence or child abuse offence, the questions that may
-- be asked of the alleged victim in cross-examination and the evidence that may be adduced about the alleged victim are subject to
-- such restrictions as may be provided for in rules made by the Minister under 154A(2).  The restrictions are in those rules, which
-- are not encoded.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 154A(1) and (2)"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 154A restrictions apply to the questions and evidence about the alleged victim` IF
    q's `the accused is charged with committing a sexual offence or child abuse offence`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 154A(2)"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the question is permitted by the section 154A rules` IF
    REFUSE "section 154A(2): the restrictions on cross-examination of and evidence about an alleged victim, their exceptions and the applications concerning them are in rules made by the Minister, which are not encoded"

-- 155: when a witness has been asked and has answered a question relevant only insofar as it tends to shake the
-- witness's credit by injuring character, no evidence may be given to contradict the witness; but if the answer is false the
-- witness may afterwards be charged with giving false evidence.  Exception 1: if the witness is asked whether previously convicted
-- of any crime and denies it, evidence may be given of the conviction.  Exception 2: if asked a question tending to impeach the
-- witness's impartiality and the witness denies the facts suggested, the witness may be contradicted.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 155 and Exceptions 1 and 2"
GIVEN q IS A `Credibility Question Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `evidence may be given to contradict the witness's answer` IF
        NOT q's `the witness has been asked and has answered a question relevant only insofar as it tends to shake the witness's credit by injuring character`
    OR  q's `the question was whether the witness has been previously convicted of a crime and the witness denies it`
    OR  q's `the question tended to impeach the witness's impartiality and the witness answered by denying the facts suggested`

§ `156 to 160A. Own witness, impeaching credit and corroboration`

-- 156: the court may in its discretion permit the person who calls a witness to put any questions that might be put in
-- cross-examination by the adverse party.  A discretion, inert here.

-- 157: the credit of a witness may be impeached by the adverse party or, with the consent of the court, by the party who calls the
-- witness, (a) by the evidence of persons who testify that from their knowledge of the witness they believe the witness unworthy of
-- credit; (b) by proof that the witness has been bribed, has accepted the offer of a bribe or has received any other corrupt
-- inducement to give evidence; (c) by proof of former statements inconsistent with any part of the witness's evidence which is
-- liable to be contradicted.  This is the impeachment "in the manner provided in section 157" in CPC s 259(1)(b).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 157(a), (b) and (c)"
GIVEN i IS AN `Impeachment and Corroboration Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the credit of the witness may be impeached under section 157` IF
        (   i's `the credit of the witness is impeached by the adverse party`
         OR i's `the credit of the witness is impeached by the party who calls the witness with the consent of the court`)
    AND (   i's `the evidence is of persons who testify that from their knowledge of the witness they believe the witness unworthy of credit`
         OR i's `the evidence is proof that the witness has been bribed, has accepted the offer of a bribe, or has received another corrupt inducement to give evidence`
         OR i's `the evidence is proof of former statements inconsistent with a part of the witness's evidence which is liable to be contradicted`)

-- 158(1): when a witness whom it is intended to corroborate gives evidence of a relevant fact, the witness may be questioned as to
-- other circumstances observed at or near the time or place if the court is of the opinion that they, if proved, would corroborate the
-- testimony.  158(2): any rule of law or practice whereby in criminal proceedings the evidence of one witness is incapable of
-- corroborating the evidence of another witness is abrogated.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 158(1)"
GIVEN i IS AN `Impeachment and Corroboration Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the witness may be questioned as to corroborating circumstances under section 158(1)` IF
        i's `the witness gives evidence of a relevant fact and is questioned as to other circumstances observed at or near the time or place of that fact`
    AND i's `the court is of the opinion that those circumstances, if proved, would corroborate the testimony of the witness as to the relevant fact`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 158(2)"
GIVEN i IS AN `Impeachment and Corroboration Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the evidence of one witness is capable of corroborating the evidence of another in criminal proceedings` IF
    i's `the proceedings are criminal proceedings and the corroborating evidence is the evidence of another witness`

-- 159: in order to corroborate the testimony of a witness, any former statement made by the witness, whether written or verbal, on
-- oath or in ordinary conversation, relating to the same fact at or about the time when the fact took place, or before any authority
-- legally competent to investigate the fact, may be proved.  A report to the police is a statement of this kind where it is the
-- witness's own former statement; section 8(2) illustrations (j) and (k) separately make the fact and circumstances of a prompt complaint
-- relevant as conduct.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 159"
GIVEN p IS A `Previous Statement Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the former statement may be proved to corroborate the testimony under section 159` IF
        p's `the witness whom it is intended to corroborate has given testimony`
    AND p's `the former statement relates to the same fact`
    AND p's `the former statement was made at or about the time when the fact took place, or before an authority legally competent to investigate the fact`

-- 160: whenever a statement relevant under section 32 or 33 is proved, all matters may be proved either to contradict or corroborate it
-- or to impeach or confirm the credit of the person by whom it was made, which might have been proved if that person had been called and
-- had denied on cross-examination the truth of the matter suggested.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 160"
GIVEN i IS AN `Impeachment and Corroboration Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `matters may be proved to contradict, corroborate, impeach or confirm the statement under section 160` IF
    i's `a statement relevant under section 32 or 33 has been proved`

-- 160A: for the purpose of any rule of law or practice that requires evidence to be corroborated or regulates the manner in which
-- uncorroborated evidence is treated, (a) a statement admissible by virtue of section 32(1) is not capable of corroborating evidence
-- given by the maker of the statement; and (b) a statement admissible by virtue of section 32(1)(b) is not capable of corroborating evidence
-- given by the person who originally supplied the information from which it was made.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 160A(a)"
GIVEN i IS AN `Impeachment and Corroboration Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the section 32(1) statement is incapable of corroborating the maker's evidence under section 160A(a)` IF
        i's `the statement is admissible by virtue of section 32(1)`
    AND i's `the evidence to be corroborated was given by the maker of the statement`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 160A(b)"
GIVEN i IS AN `Impeachment and Corroboration Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the section 32(1)(b) statement is incapable of corroborating the supplier's evidence under section 160A(b)` IF
        i's `the statement is admissible by virtue of section 32(1)(b)`
    AND i's `the evidence to be corroborated was given by the person who originally supplied the information from which the statement was made`

§ `161 to 163. Refreshing memory`

-- 161(1): a witness may while under examination refresh the witness's memory by referring to any writing made by the witness at the time of
-- the transaction, or so soon afterwards that the court considers it likely that the transaction was then fresh in the witness's memory.
-- 161(2): the witness may also refer to such a writing made by another person and read by the witness within that time, if when the witness
-- read it the witness knew it to be correct.  161(3): with the court's permission the witness may refer to a copy if the court is satisfied
-- there is sufficient reason for the non-production of the original.  161(4): an expert may refresh memory by professional treatises.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 161(1) to (4)"
GIVEN r IS A `Refreshing Memory Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the witness may refresh memory by the writing under section 161` IF
        r's `the writing was made by the witness at the time of the transaction, or so soon afterwards that the court considers it likely that the transaction was then fresh in the witness's memory`
    OR  (   r's `the writing was made by another person and was read by the witness within that time`
        AND r's `the witness knew it to be correct when the witness read it`)
    OR  (   r's `the witness refers to a copy of the document with the permission of the court`
        AND r's `the court is satisfied that there is sufficient reason for the non-production of the original`)
    OR  r's `the witness is an expert referring to a professional treatise`

-- 162: a witness may also testify to facts mentioned in such a document although the witness has no specific recollection of them, if the
-- witness is sure that the facts were correctly recorded in the document.  163: any writing referred to under 161 or 162 must be produced
-- and shown to the adverse party if the party requires it, and the party may cross-examine on it.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 162"
GIVEN r IS A `Refreshing Memory Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the witness may testify to the facts recorded in the document under section 162` IF
        `the witness may refresh memory by the writing under section 161` r
    AND r's `the witness has no specific recollection of the facts but is sure that the facts were correctly recorded in the document`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 163"
GIVEN r IS A `Refreshing Memory Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the writing must be produced and shown to the adverse party under section 163` IF
        `the witness may refresh memory by the writing under section 161` r
    AND r's `the adverse party requires the writing to be produced and shown`

§ `164 to 169. Documents, the court's powers, and improper admission or rejection`

-- 164(1): a witness summoned to produce a document must, if it is in the witness's possession or power, bring it to court despite any
-- objection to its production or admissibility.  164(2): the validity of the objection is decided by the court.  164(3): the court may inspect
-- the document unless it refers to affairs of State.  164(4): the court may direct a translator to keep contents secret; breach is an offence
-- under section 166 of the Penal Code 1871.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 164(1) to (3)"
GIVEN c IS A `Court Power Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the witness must bring the document to court under section 164(1)` IF
        c's `the witness was summoned to produce a document`
    AND c's `the document is in the witness's possession or power`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 164(3)"
GIVEN c IS A `Court Power Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may inspect the document under section 164(3)` IF
    NOT c's `the document refers to affairs of State`

-- 165: when a party calls for a document which the party gave the other notice to produce, and it is produced and inspected by the party, the
-- party is bound to give it as evidence if the party producing it requires and it is relevant.  166: when a party refuses to produce a document
-- which the party had notice to produce, the party cannot afterwards use it as evidence without the consent of the other party or the order of
-- the court.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 165"
GIVEN r IS A `Refreshing Memory Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the party is bound to give the document as evidence under section 165` IF
        r's `the party called for a document that the party gave the other party notice to produce, and it was produced and inspected`
    AND r's `the party producing it requires the party who called for it to give it as evidence`
    AND r's `the document is relevant`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 166"
GIVEN r IS A `Refreshing Memory Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the party may afterwards use the document as evidence under section 166` IF
        NOT r's `the party refused to produce a document that the party had notice to produce`
    OR  r's `the other party consents or the court orders that the document be used`

-- 167(1): the Judge may, to discover or obtain proper proof of relevant facts, ask any question in any form at any time of any witness or of the
-- parties about any fact relevant or irrelevant, and order production of any document or thing, without objection by the parties.  167(2): the judgment
-- must be based on facts declared by the Act to be relevant and duly proved.  167(3): this section does not authorise a Judge to compel a witness to
-- answer or produce what the witness would be entitled to refuse under sections 123 to 133.  167(4): the Judge must not (a) ask a question that would be
-- improper under section 150 or 151; or (b) dispense with the primary evidence of a document, except in the cases excepted in the Act.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 167(3)"
GIVEN c IS A `Court Power Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Judge may compel the witness to answer or produce under section 167` IF
    NOT c's `the witness would be entitled under sections 123 to 133 to refuse to answer the question or produce the document if the adverse party asked or called for it`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 167(4)"
GIVEN c IS A `Court Power Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Judge may ask the question or dispense with primary evidence under section 167(4)` IF
        NOT c's `the question would be improper for any other person to ask under section 150 or 151`
    AND (   NOT c's `the Judge proposes to dispense with the primary evidence of a document`
         OR c's `the case is one excepted in the Act`)

-- 168: in cases tried with assessors, the assessors may put questions through or by permission of the Judge.  Inert here.

-- 169: the improper admission or rejection of evidence is not of itself ground for a new trial or reversal of any decision if it appears to the court
-- that, independently of the evidence objected to and admitted, there was sufficient evidence to justify the decision, or that, if the rejected evidence
-- had been received, it ought not to have varied the decision.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 169"
GIVEN c IS A `Court Power Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the improper admission or rejection of evidence is not a ground for a new trial or reversal under section 169` IF
        c's `there was sufficient evidence to justify the decision independently of the evidence objected to and admitted`
    OR  c's `the rejected evidence, if received, ought not to have varied the decision`
